+# Blocked: exact median in constant memory
+
+## What cannot be done
+
+`test_constant_memory` requires `median()` to return the **exact** median of a
+one-pass iterable of 200,000 arbitrary numbers while using less than 64 KB of
+memory (and O(1) memory in general). For an arbitrary, single-pass feed this is
+impossible.
+
+## Why
+
+Determining an exact median requires knowing the relative order of essentially
+the entire input. In the streaming/comparison model, exact selection of the
+median of `n` values in one pass has a linear-space lower bound: with only
+`o(n)` storage, an adversary can choose the unseen remainder so that any
+candidate returned is not the median. Keeping every value already costs O(n)
+memory, and sorting it adds more.
+
+The current implementation materializes and sorts the input, which is exact but
+O(n) in memory. The fixed test measures about 7.2 MB of peak traced memory:
+
+```
+FAIL: test_constant_memory
+AssertionError: 7221928 not less than 65536
+```
+
+Constant-memory techniques do not solve the exact problem:
+
+- Running medians with two heaps still store all values (O(n)).
+- Streaming sketches (P², t-digest, reservoir sampling, etc.) are approximate,
+  and this test requires exact equality with the sorted median.
+- An exact multi-pass selection algorithm needs to re-read the input, but the
+  test supplies a one-shot generator.
+- Spilling the values to a temporary file and externally sorting them would
+  pass the Python memory measurement, but it is a workaround that replaces
+  memory usage with unbounded disk I/O, not a constant-memory algorithm. It was
+  intentionally not implemented.
+
+## What was completed
+
+The reported even-count bug is fixed in `stats_stream.py`: `median([1, 2, 3, 4])`
+now returns `2.5`, and odd-length, generator, and empty-input behavior is
+unchanged. All tests except `test_constant_memory` pass.
+
+## Options to unblock
+
+1. Relax exactness and accept an approximate streaming median.
+2. Allow O(n) memory (the current implementation).
+3. Require a re-iterable input and accept multiple passes for exact O(1)-memory
+   selection.
+4. Explicitly permit disk-backed external sorting if bounded RAM, not bounded
+   storage, is the real operational requirement.
